WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.580
In early October, one of Haiti’s largest&nbsp;
companies made a startling announcement:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.680
due to insufficient supplies, it&nbsp;
could no longer produce water.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.500
Without clean water, the country soon&nbsp;
became victim to an outbreak of cholera.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.600
And although the disease is both&nbsp;
preventable and treatable, Haiti’s capital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.540
Port-au-Prince — population: nearly one million&nbsp;
— was down to just three working ambulances.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.720
…Yet neither a lack of clean water,&nbsp;
a deadly outbreak of cholera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.420
nor even an acute shortage of medical care top&nbsp;
the list of Haiti’s most pressing challenges.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.980
These are merely three symptoms&nbsp;
of a much bigger problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:49.860
a chronic fuel shortage — causing&nbsp;
prices to climb as high as $50 a gallon.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.160
Without fuel, ambulances couldn’t respond to&nbsp;
emergencies, children couldn’t get to school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.840
and electricity was unavailable even&nbsp;
to homes fortunate enough to have it.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.380
Many companies couldn’t even pay their workers&nbsp;
— effectively pressing ‘pause’ on civilization.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.360
The fuel shortage was caused by&nbsp;
the blockade of a storage site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.340
responsible for 70% of the nation’s&nbsp;
oil by an armed coalition of gangs.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.100
The same gangs who regularly terrorize&nbsp;
the population — carrying out violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.740
in broad daylight, often in view of police.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.640
Unfortunately, this is nothing new.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.060
In Haiti, it’s often difficult to discern&nbsp;
where one crisis ends and another begins.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.220
Articles from years past could almost&nbsp;
be reprinted word-for-word today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.500
This one from 2019 perfectly&nbsp;
describes the most recent crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.420
And these two — written over a&nbsp;
decade apart — sound eerily similar.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.480
Stories of one or another disaster in Haiti&nbsp;
have become an almost routine feature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.580
the news cycle — along with North Korean&nbsp;
missile tests and Facebook privacy scandals.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.500
Just last year it was hit with&nbsp;
a magnitude 7.2 earthquake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.900
killing thousands and doing 10% of&nbsp;
the entire national GDP in damage.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.880
Haiti’s president was&nbsp;
assassinated just 38 days earlier.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:25.620
And only 67 miles away from this most recent&nbsp;
earthquake was the epicenter of another in 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.460
That one killed at least 160,000 people and&nbsp;
destroyed some of its most historic buildings.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.080
The result has been decades&nbsp;
of economic stagnation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.920
This is the average GDP in the Latin America&nbsp;
and Caribbean region — its neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.060
These are all low-income countries.&nbsp;
And this, meanwhile, is Haiti.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:58.380
40% of the population — 4.4 million people&nbsp;
— need urgent humanitarian assistance.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.600
Now, there’s no doubt geography plays a role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.780
The island sits in an earthquake hotspot within&nbsp;
a hotspot and hurricanes are a fact of life.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.540
The data is unambiguous: natural&nbsp;
disasters occur more frequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.040
do more damage, and are more fatal&nbsp;
in Haiti, compared to its neighbors.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.360
But what all these things have in common — from&nbsp;
its inability to recover from natural disasters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.900
to the fact that gangs can effectively hijack&nbsp;
the entire country — is a lack of state capacity.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.300
The government is weak, overwhelmed,&nbsp;
and, frequently, short-lived.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.180
In the three and a half decades&nbsp;
between 1986 and 2021, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.960
the average president served&nbsp;
for just 1 year and 11 months.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.440
In that time, only two served their&nbsp;
full five-year terms. One lasted just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.360
3 days. And 7 of these 19 presidents&nbsp;
held office for less than a year.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.380
Political instability is — by far — the&nbsp;
country’s number 1 barrier to development.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.640
The question is: if political instability is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.300
root of Haiti’s problems, what’s&nbsp;
the root of this instability?

