00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:40.080
When the thirteen colonies declared independence&nbsp;
in 1776, the United States was overshadowed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.520
economically, by one of its&nbsp;
tiny Southern neighbors… Haiti.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:51.480
Then a French colony, Haiti was the source of&nbsp;
75% of the world’s sugar and half of its coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.820
despite being roughly the size of Massachusetts.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:05:01.200
It was, in fact, the richest colony in the New&nbsp;
World, though, of course, this immense wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.880
did not reach the almost million slaves who,&nbsp;
at one point or another, were brought there.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:11.520
But what their French colonizers didn’t&nbsp;
realize is that many of these slaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.600
were former soldiers, captured in&nbsp;
battle throughout central Africa.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.780
And when the French Revolution reached&nbsp;
the shores of Haiti in 1791, they rose up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.220
declaring the first independent Caribbean&nbsp;
state and first free black republic.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:33.420
To symbolize this, the republic’s first flag was&nbsp;
based on France’s, with the white stripe removed.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.860
There was, however, one major problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.900
A bit of “small print” that would haunt&nbsp;
the country for centuries to come.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.300
Although Haiti had triumphed in battle&nbsp;
against the French, Spanish, English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.820
and even Napoleon himself, there was&nbsp;
one thing it still needed: recognition.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:00.120
For France, losing control of its most&nbsp;
profitable colony was a national embarrassment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:07.020
For years it refused even to speak the name&nbsp;
“Haiti”, pretending as if nothing ever happened.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.460
But without recognition, Haiti was effectively&nbsp;
stuck in limbo — unable to sign treaties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.880
send diplomats, or feel secure in its sovereignty.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.420
So, having exhausted all other options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:26.520
Haiti’s president made a suggestion:&nbsp;
What if it could simply pay?

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:33.540
For years, the two countries negotiated back&nbsp;
and forth until one day France decided enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:40.920
was enough. Haiti would owe it 112 million&nbsp;
Francs — about 560 million in today’s dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.940
French goods would be subject to&nbsp;
only half the standard tariffs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:49.920
and, in return, it would finally&nbsp;
recognize the country’s existence.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.140
But this time it wasn’t asking. If Haiti refused,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.020
it threatened to bombard the&nbsp;
capital with cannon fire.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.720
Haiti had no choice.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.460
While many empires have been&nbsp;
called on to pay reparations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:12.840
only in Haiti did slaves pay their former masters&nbsp;
— forced, essentially, to buy their own bodies.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.540
France didn’t just steal much-needed&nbsp;
resources from this newly independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:22.080
republic — resources it used to build the Eiffel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.020
Tower — it also trapped the country&nbsp;
in a payday loan-like cycle of debt.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:32.760
It had no choice but to&nbsp;
take on more and more loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.760
often with predatory interest rates and fees.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:43.320
In 1838, half of Haiti’s annual budget&nbsp;
was spent on the military. Less than 1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:48.000
on education. And about 30%&nbsp;
servicing its national debt.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:55.620
By the turn of the century, that 30% had&nbsp;
become 50. And in 1914, a staggering 80%.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.880
It’s been estimated that without this debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.720
the nation’s per capita income&nbsp;
might be six times higher today!

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:10.440
…And yet even this steep sacrifice did not&nbsp;
earn Haiti a seat at the international table.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:16.620
More than fifty years after it declared&nbsp;
independence, two important powers still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:20.400
refused to recognize it: the&nbsp;
Vatican and the United States.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:24.420
To do so, places like America and Britain thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.840
might inspire similar&nbsp;
revolts among slaves at home.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:35.460
Only in 1862, after Southern states left the&nbsp;
Union, did the U.S. officially recognize it.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:42.300
Little did Haiti know, American involvement would&nbsp;
soon become much more a curse than a blessing.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.680
Between 1870 and 1905, the share of&nbsp;
U.S. imports to Haiti nearly doubled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.620
from less than 40% to over 70.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:00.060
The former French colony effectively&nbsp;
freed itself from the grips of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:05.640
empire only to replace it with another.&nbsp;
And this new one was no less greedy.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:11.520
From Washington’s point of view, Haiti was&nbsp;
becoming far too dominated by German merchants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.760
who weren’t afraid to use their&nbsp;
wealth to support revolution.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.420
On the eve of World War I, Americans weren’t too&nbsp;
keen on inviting the Germans into their backyard.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.440
They also had an interest in&nbsp;
opening up Haiti’s economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.720
which, as a safeguard against a return to&nbsp;
slavery, was largely off-limits to foreigners.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.420
In reality, the Germans likely had&nbsp;
no intention of taking over Haiti.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:41.340
But it didn’t matter — the United States&nbsp;
saw its golden opportunity to kill two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:46.200
birds with one stone. To drive out the&nbsp;
Germans while converting a perennial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:50.820
source of instability just 600 miles from&nbsp;
Florida into an obedient client state.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.240
The U.S. government, at the time, didn’t&nbsp;
actually know much about Haiti. But it soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.160
found an ally more than happy to fill&nbsp;
its vacuum of knowledge: Wall Street.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:10.200
To American corporations, Haiti represented a&nbsp;
giant untapped market — if only it were stable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.480
The only thing that could stabilize&nbsp;
it, they decided, was U.S. occupation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:20.820
And the only thing that could compel&nbsp;
U.S. occupation was an acute crisis.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.120
So, they set out to create one.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.760
The National City Bank of New York&nbsp;
— now known as Citibank — set its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.240
plan in motion by purchasing a&nbsp;
majority stake in a Haitian bank.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.180
But not just any ordinary bank.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:41.820
As a private, for-profit French&nbsp;
corporation, it could simply be bought.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:46.500
But it also just so happened to&nbsp;
serve as Haiti’s national treasury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.920
with a monopoly on printing&nbsp;
currency, holding tax revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.780
and even commercial lending — services&nbsp;
it provided to the government for a fee.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.820
Whoever owned it, in other&nbsp;
words, effectively owned Haiti.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.320
All the Americans had to do to get what&nbsp;
they wanted was withhold the government’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.360
money. Or just outright steal it.&nbsp;
And that’s exactly what they did.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.080
Their opportunity came after another investor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.700
James MacDonald, promised to build a railway&nbsp;
connecting two of Haiti’s largest cities.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:23.220
In exchange, he was granted the rights to&nbsp;
12 miles of land on each side of the tracks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.440
where he planned on building banana plantations.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.900
To locals, this sounded suspiciously&nbsp;
like a return to the exact kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.440
foreign-owned export economy the&nbsp;
country was founded to prevent.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.860
These alarm bells led to an uprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.400
and seven presidents came and went&nbsp;
in a matter of just four years.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:46.140
Although this was far from the&nbsp;
most violence Haiti had ever seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:52.260
this time American property was “at risk” —&nbsp;
providing the perfect pretext for an invasion.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:59.160
In 1914, U.S. Marines landed in Port-au-Prince,&nbsp;
stole millions of dollars in gold from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:04.500
national treasury, and sailed away as if they&nbsp;
hadn’t just committed 17th-century armed piracy.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.920
A year later, they returned. This time,&nbsp;
they stayed for nearly two decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:15.360
during which the U.S. installed a&nbsp;
puppet government, declared martial law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.880
and re-established a form of forced labor.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.960
Beating out the French for the&nbsp;
title of Haiti’s biggest villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:26.280
was a near-impossible task but&nbsp;
the Americans somehow managed.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:32.220
Far from stabilizing the country, laying the&nbsp;
groundwork for rapid economic development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:36.420
as intended, U.S. involvement&nbsp;
had an unexpected consequence.

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:41.400
Not only did it centralize power and&nbsp;
help normalize political violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:44.340
but it also undermined the&nbsp;
government’s legitimacy.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.420
How could Haitians see their&nbsp;
leaders as being legitimate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.220
when they answered first to Washington?

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.080
Now, to be clear, the Haitian&nbsp;
government has historically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.980
been just as much a perpetrator&nbsp;
as a victim of this broken system.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.340
Corruption is rampant and violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:07.260
at best, tolerated, and all too&nbsp;
often, outright state-sponsored.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:14.040
Nobody — in or outside of Haiti — trusts&nbsp;
its government. And for good reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.100
But this creates its own problems.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.540
Take, for example, the&nbsp;
devastating 2010 earthquake.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:28.380
Unlike most foreign natural disasters, it not&nbsp;
only captured but sustained the world’s attention.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:35.940
Millions donated to relief efforts and&nbsp;
$2.29 billion in aid was promised in total.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:42.960
Suddenly Haiti was being overwhelmed with&nbsp;
money, and, for once, genuine goodwill.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:46.920
The only question was where to put it.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.240
When the earthquake struck, the&nbsp;
government of Haiti was so weak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.900
that UN peacekeepers were already in&nbsp;
the country on a different mission.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:59.940
It not only wiped out virtually&nbsp;
every public building but also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.940
very UN building which would’ve&nbsp;
provided earthquake relief.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:09.360
Just 1% of humanitarian aid and&nbsp;
23% of recovery funds between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.300
2010 and ‘11 were directed to the government.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:19.560
Instead, nearly every last penny went,&nbsp;
as it always does in Haiti, to NGOs.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:26.100
And as more and more money bypasses the&nbsp;
government, it becomes weaker and weaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:29.100
ensuring the next crisis is even deadlier.

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:35.040
It’s clear that Haiti will never&nbsp;
escape this cycle until it has strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.140
capable, and competent leadership.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.940
But it’s also become abundantly&nbsp;
clear that foreign intervention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:45.660
is more likely to aggravate&nbsp;
than relieve Haiti’s suffering.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:51.720
That, although the source of its challenges&nbsp;
may be foreign, the cure can’t be.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:57.420
That, although geography may predispose&nbsp;
the country to frequent natural disasters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.440
the Haiti of 2022 was far from predetermined.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.980
“Natural” explanations for Haiti’s struggles are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:11.220
simply an excuse for centuries of abuse by&nbsp;
foreign powers. The same storm, after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:16.020
can be catastrophic in one place and&nbsp;
only mildly inconvenient in another.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:20.760
And the scientific reasons for that&nbsp;
are, actually, quite interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

